2d Nebula Shader by flytrap
https://godotshaders.com/shader/2d-nebula-shader/
Author page: https://godotshaders.com/author/flytrap/

The star drawing in this shader is credited by its author to the "Stars shader"
by gerardogc2378, https://godotshaders.com/shader/stars-shader/ , which is also
published under CC0 (see below).

LICENCE STATEMENT RELIED ON

Where it appears: the licence notice printed under the shader code on the
shader's own page, https://godotshaders.com/shader/2d-nebula-shader/ (the page
also carries the licence tag "CC0"). Verbatim:

  "The shader code and all code snippets in this post are under CC0 license and
  can be used freely without the author's permission. Images and videos, and
  assets depicted in those, do not fall under this license. For more info, see
  our License terms."

The site's terms, https://godotshaders.com/license/ , say that when a shader is
submitted "the shader code and other code snippets in the post will be under the
license that you choose", and that the submitter agrees "the code you submit is
your own or already considered free to share". The same notice, word for word,
appears on the credited Stars shader page, https://godotshaders.com/shader/stars-shader/ .

The notice covers the shader code only. The page's featured image, screenshots
and live preview are not covered, and none of them is part of this scene.

CHANGES FROM THE ORIGINAL

Ported to Metal Shading Language for MacOS Live Wallpaper. The author's
algorithm, constants and colours are unchanged. Changes:

  - The Godot wrapper (shader_type, uniform, fragment(), UV, TIME, COLOR) is
    removed, and the exposed uniforms are fixed at their default values.
  - UV is rebuilt from the pixel position: origin at the top left as in Godot,
    the shorter side of the screen spans 0..1, so the picture is not stretched.
  - Slowed for a desktop background. The original scrolls the clouds by
    0.03*T*sin(0.07*T) with T = 5*TIME, so the offset and the speed grow without
    limit. Here T = 0.25*TIME and the growing factor T is replaced by a constant
    (100, what the original has after 20 seconds), so the clouds drift on a slow
    bounded loop. The cloud "tide" and the star twinkle run at a quarter of the
    original rate.
  - The final colour is clamped to 0..1, as Godot's 8-bit framebuffer does.
  - The star spikes divide by the distance to the star's centre lines; that
    distance is kept at 0.0001 or more so no pixel divides by zero.
  - The unused fbm() function and the unused texture lookup are dropped.
  - Renamed constants (size, prob, zoomScale, starscale) to avoid clashes.

